WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.840
Why do some young people
from really tough backgrounds

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:06.520
beat the odds?

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.800
And what can we all learn from them?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:15.360
When I was a child,
I was bounced from children's home

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:18.200
to children's home.
Yeah, it was tough.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.600
But I made it as a writer

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.040
and as a poet.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:27.600
So this subject, above all,
is very close to my heart.

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:40.840
There are no simple answers,

00:00:40.880 --> 00:00:43.600
but we do have
some really interesting clues.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:45.720
Some come from a major study,

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:47.920
which has been following
12,000 children

00:00:47.960 --> 00:00:49.720
in four different countries,

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:51.960
over a period of 20 years.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:55.400
The Young Lives team,
led by the University of Oxford,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:01:00.400
tracks children in Ethiopia,
Peru, Vietnam and India.

00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:03.160
They looked at those
who'd managed to beat the odds

00:01:03.200 --> 00:01:06.440
and pulled out
a few common threads.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.120
Here are three key ones.

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:16.000
I didn't have family around me,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.560
and the absence of it actually
gives you an opportunity

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:21.480
to see just how important

00:01:21.520 --> 00:01:24.760
these supportive relationships are
for a child.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:28.440
I saw them
because I didn't have them.

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:30.120
Think of a baby
just learning to crawl.

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:33.360
They only go so far
before they look back.

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:36.120
When I looked back,
there was nobody there.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:41.280
However tenuous, having somebody
who's on your side,

00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:43.280
it's really important.

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.840
For me, I didn't realise it
at the time,

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.240
but the truth is
my social worker was that person.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.160
He didn't feel like a role model.

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.840
He didn't feel like a friend.

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:55.040
But he was something more.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:58.320
He was a person
keeping his binoculars

00:01:58.360 --> 00:02:01.280
focused on my emotional state.

00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:05.080
And that -
that proved pivotal to me.

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:13.040
The second key element is
the importance of a safety net.

00:02:13.080 --> 00:02:17.600
Studies in the UK have consistently
shown a link between poverty

00:02:17.640 --> 00:02:19.720
and poor outcomes for kids.

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.120
Children who experience
persistent poverty

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:24.920
are four times more likely

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:29.040
to be in the bottom 10%
in vocabulary aged five.

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.160
Small things can make
a big difference.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:36.280
Like the warmth of the relationship
between a parent and a child.

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.040
Reading to kids.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:42.200
And having a safety net
in those early years

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:43.840
is so important.

00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:48.600
In Ethiopia and Peru,
the Young Lives team found children

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.760
whose parents had received
government support

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.360
for basics like food
when they were very young

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:57.680
had significantly better long-term
memory and cognitive skills

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:00.880
at the age of 12
than those who hadn't.

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:04.120
The more disadvantaged the family,
the greater the impact.

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:06.400
And there's similar evidence
from the US,

00:03:06.440 --> 00:03:10.760
where the Baby's First Years study
looked at the impact of giving money

00:03:10.800 --> 00:03:13.720
to the poorest families
in four cities.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:18.960
They gave $20 a month to one set
of families, the control group,

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.240
and $333 a month to the other group.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:27.080
They then scanned the brains
of the babies at the age of one,

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:30.120
and just look at the difference.

00:03:30.160 --> 00:03:32.120
The images on the left

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:34.400
show the babies
from the high-cash group

00:03:34.440 --> 00:03:37.000
and those on the right
from the low-cash group.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.480
The babies whose mothers

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.440
received the high-cash gift

00:03:40.480 --> 00:03:42.400
showed significantly more activity

00:03:42.440 --> 00:03:43.640
in parts of the brain

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:45.200
associated with language

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.440
and cognitive skills.

00:03:47.480 --> 00:03:49.200
Wow.

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:54.920
When I look back at my younger self,

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:57.400
all I wanted was compassion.

00:03:57.440 --> 00:04:01.720
And second chances
are about just that - compassion.

00:04:01.760 --> 00:04:05.040
I was given a book of poetry
by a teacher in the children's home

00:04:05.080 --> 00:04:07.480
when I was 13 years of age.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:10.080
But it wasn't just the book
of poetry which helped me -

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.640
though it did immensely.

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.600
It was also the fact that
I was worth giving a book to.

00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:23.440
I was lucky because
I found the thing I love - poetry.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:25.000
And regardless of your upbringing,

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.040
that's one of the greatest things
that a child, or a person,

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:29.840
can ever find -

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:32.040
what it is that they love.

